# Gym Notebook's site blocks for the tracker's Caddy, the only process on the
# server's ports 80/443. Copied to /opt/edge/sites/ on the server, where the
# tracker's Caddyfile imports /etc/caddy/sites/*.caddy (research R4). The
# behavior it promises is contracts/edge-http.md.
#
# Changing it is an admin step, never a deploy: copy the file and run the
# tracker's `caddy reload`, which validates the whole config first and keeps
# the old one if this file is broken, so a mistake here cannot take the
# tracker down.
#
# Upstreams are resolved when Caddy dials, over the `edge` network, so a
# stopped gym-api gives 502 on our hostnames only.
#
# No `trusted_proxies`, on purpose: Caddy then replaces any client
# X-Forwarded-For with the TCP peer, which gym-api trusts as the client
# address for rate limits (FORWARDED_HEADERS_ENABLED in compose.prod.yml,
# research R5). If Cloudflare's proxy is ever turned on, this and the privacy
# inventory change first.
#
# No `log` directive either: no per-request access line for our hostnames,
# keeping the decision behind `access_log off` in frontend/nginx.conf (plan
# D10). Caddy's error log stays on.

gymnotebook.fit {
	reverse_proxy gym-web:80
}

api.gymnotebook.fit {
	reverse_proxy gym-api:8080
}
